[metadata]
title: CSS Masonry is Finally Native: Goodbye Complex Grid Hacks and Third-Party Libraries
channel: @octopusinc_
duration: 12:05
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A2EmrQ3nV88
upload_date: 20260718
transcript_source: captions+frames
route: visual

---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, I want to start with something you have almost certainly fought with. You\'re building a photo gallery or a wall of little cards, and the images are all different heights. Some are tall, some are short. And you want them to stack up nice and tight like a brick'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wall with no ugly empty gaps between them. That look has a name. It\'s called a masonry layout. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And for years, getting it to actually work meant picking one of two bad options.'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Either you reached for a heavy JavaScript library, something like Isotope or Macy. Or you glued together a pile of fragile CSS hacks [music] and just hoped they held. So today, we\'re done with both. Stop'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wrestling with bloated libraries and fragile hacks just to build a simple staggered grid. For years, we compromised the structure of our page and the performance of our page all for this one layout. But that'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'era is officially over. Because today, we\'re going to build that exact same [music] layout with no library, no hack, and honestly, almost no code at all. Just one new line of'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'native CSS. So let\'s understand how we got stuck here in the first place, and then [music] how this new thing actually works starting from the ground up. So first, let\'s be crystal clear about'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what we\'re even trying to build and why it was ever hard. Picture a normal CSS grid. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; When you make a grid, the browser builds a strict table. Neat rows, neat columns like a spreadsheet. And every item has'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to line up on those row lines. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now that is perfect when your items are all the same size. But the second your cards are different heights, you\'ve got a problem. Because the whole row has to wait for'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the tallest card in it. So a short card sitting next to a tall one leaves this big awkward blank space underneath it until the next row starts. Multiply that across a whole page and you get this'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ugly, gappy, ragged mess. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; That is the exact thing a masonry layout fixes. It ignores those rigid rows, and instead lets each card slide straight up to fill the gap above it. Tight,'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; staggered, no wasted space. Simple to describe, it turned out to be surprisingly hard to actually do. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, how did we do it before today? Let\'s look at the two old ways, because'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understanding why they hurt is the whole reason the new way is such a big deal. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The first old trick was a pure CSS one, using a property called column-count. And at first glance, it looks like it'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; It chops your content into columns, and the heights stagger. But it has one fatal [music] flaw. Column-count fills the first column all the way to the bottom, then starts the second column, then the third.'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, your items flow top to bottom, then jump back up to the top of the next column. That means the visual order does not match the order of your actual content. Item number two might end up at the'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bottom of the screen, while item number four sits up at the top. For a screen reader, or for someone tabbing through with a keyboard, that is genuinely broken. So, that hack was always a compromise.'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The second old way was to bring in a JavaScript library. And this is the one I really want you to understand, because the cost is hidden. A library like Isotope does this. It waits for the page to load, then it'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'measures the exact height of every single card, and then it manually positions each one, giving every card a hard pixel coordinate, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; like top 200 pixels, left 300 pixels. And it has to redo that entire'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'calculation every time the window resizes. Now, why is that expensive? Here\'s the key idea. The browser has one main thread. Think of it as one single worker who has to do [music] everything. Run your'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'JavaScript, calculate the layout, and paint pixels to the screen all in the same line. When your masonry library jumps in and measures and re-positions hundreds of cards on that one thread, that worker'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gets swamped. The page stutters. And worse, because the library only fixes the positions after the page is already drawn once, your content visibly jumps around as it loads.'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, that\'s the mess we\'ve lived in. Ugly gaps or broken order or a performance tax you pay on the main [music] thread. For years, there was no clean answer. Okay, so now, at last, the fix. And I'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise, it is almost anti-climactic [music] in the best possible way. The browser folks finally built masonry directly into CSS. The engine does it for you now, natively. No library'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'required. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Quick, honest note, because this is brand new and the names are still settling, you\'ll also hear this feature called grid lines. And that name is actually a really good hint about how it'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works. So, hold that thought. Let\'s see the one line that does [music] it. Take our gallery. You already have a normal grid. You\'ve said, "Display grid." And you\'ve set up your columns. Let\'s say three of them.'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, here is the entire trick. You add one line. You set grid-template-rows to the value masonry. That\'s it. The moment you do that, every card slides up and fills the gap above it.'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And your whole layout snaps into that perfect tight staggered wall. One property. No library, no measuring, no pixel math. The browser just does it. But, I don\'t want you to just copy a'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'magic line. I want you to actually understand what the browser is [music] doing under the hood, because it\'s beautifully simple. Remember that new name, grid lines. Here\'s the idea. Normally, a grid forces both columns and'

[05:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] rows. When you say grid-template-rows: masonry, you\'re telling the browser, "Stop forcing rows [music] on me. Keep my columns, but treat them as open lanes, like empty lanes at a bowling alley, that can [music] just keep'

[05:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'filling downward." Then the browser walks through your cards one at a time in order. [music] And for each card, it asks one tiny question. "Which lane [music] is currently the shortest?" And it drops the card right there, into whichever'

[06:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'column is currently the [music] least full. That is the entire algorithm. Picture a bricklayer who always places the next brick wherever the wall is lowest. Do that card after card, and the wall stays even, and every gap gets filled.'

[06:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Compare that to the old column count hack, which poured items all the way down one column before even looking at the next. Masonry instead moves across the front of all the columns together, always'

[06:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feeding the shortest one. That one difference is why the order stays sane, and the gaps disappear at the same time. Now, sometimes you want to say in how that flow behaves, and there\'s a'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'property for that, too. It\'s called masonry-auto-flow, and it controls [music] a genuine little trade-off. By default, the browser packs items for the tightest [music] possible fit,'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'always dropping the next card into the shortest lane. That gives you the most beautiful, gap-free wall. But packing for the tightest fit can occasionally shuffle two items slightly out of their strict source order, if a'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'later item happens to fit a gap better. So, if keeping the exact original order matters more to you than a perfectly even bottom edge, you can switch masonry-auto-flow to keep strict order instead.'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Tightest wall, or most faithful order. One property lets you choose. And just so you know, since this is all so new, the exact keyword names here are still being finalized across browsers,'

[07:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] so that\'s a spot to double-check the docs when you build it. And here\'s the part people always worry about with a new layout tool. What about spacing? And what about the special items? &gt;&gt; [music]'

[07:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Good news on both. Spacing just works. You use the exact same gap property you already use on a normal grid. Set gap to 1 rem and every card gets clean, consistent spacing in both [music] directions, and the masonry math handles'

[08:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it automatically. You don\'t do anything special. [music] And for special items, say you have one featured card or an ad slot that needs to span [music] across two or three columns, you can still place it explicitly the normal grid way with grid'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] column. The browser handles those placed items first, locks them in, and then flows all the regular masonry cards neatly around them. So, your hero content and your ad slots still behave'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly how you\'d expect. So, let\'s talk about what you actually gain from all this beyond just cleaner code, because this is where it really pays off. First, performance, and specifically a thing'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'called Cumulative Layout Shift, or CLS. CLS is a real score the browser tracks, and it measures how much your content jumps around while the page is loading. Users hate that jump. You felt it. You'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go to tap a button and the page shoves it out from under your finger. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Remember, the old JavaScript libraries caused exactly that because they repositioned everything after the first paint.'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Native masonry has none of that. The browser lays it out correctly the very first time, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; so there\'s no jump, and your CLS score drops right down. Second, [music] accessibility. Because native masonry keeps your real content order untouched,'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a screen reader and a keyboard user move through your cards in the true logical order, not the scrambled order that the old column count hack produced. [music] You get the nice visual and the correct'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'order for free at the same time. And third, the simplest win of all, you get to delete the library. That whole chunk of JavaScript you were shipping to every visitor, gone. Your bundle gets smaller, your page loads faster, and that matters'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most on a phone, on a slower connection, which is most of your users. Now, before you go rip out every library today, let me be straight with you because this is brand new. As of right now, in 2026, Safari has shipped this natively, and'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s genuinely there. But, Chrome and Firefox are still finishing it up behind experimental flags, and they\'re expected to ship it fully a bit later this year. So, how do you use something this good'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in production [music] safely right now? You use a feature query. A feature query is basically an if statement [music] for CSS. Using the supports rule, you ask the browser one question. Hey, do you'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually understand masonry? If the browser says yes, it gets the beautiful native masonry layout. And if it says no, that browser just falls back to [music] a normal plain grid, which still looks completely fine, just without the'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stagger. Nobody gets a broken page. So, you can ship this today, and it simply gets better on its own, automatically, as Chrome and Firefox catch up. And looking a little further ahead, this'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pairs up with other new grid features [music] like subgrid, which means soon you\'ll be able to build really rich, art-directed nested layouts that used to be almost impossible, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; all in pure CSS, all native. So, let me give you the whole story back in one breath. A masonry layout is that tight, staggered, [music] gap-free wall of different sized cards. For years, we faked [music] it either with a CSS hack'

[11:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that broke our content order, or with a JavaScript library that measured and repositioned everything on the main thread, [music] hurting performance and making the page jump. Now, the browser does it natively. [music] You add one line, grid template'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rows masonry, and the engine simply drops each card into the shortest lane all by itself. You keep your correct order, you kill the layout shift, and you delete the library entirely. Wrap it'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a feature query today and you get the best of both worlds while the other browsers catch up. That is native CSS masonry. If this finally made layouts click for you, do me one small favor and'

[11:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subscribe. We go deep on exactly one concept every single week, and I\'ll see you in the next class.'
